 of the  universe but of course as I said this is  is something we are trying to work out  and first we are planning to construct a  theory to a subsystem of the universe to  find predictions about certain other  problems the cosmological problem is is  a very interesting one by something that  is going to be worked out in the future  for doing the physics job you have to  solve problems that come up in your  understanding of physical reality the  way you see it around you and in a way  it's it's very simple the way you do  that is just you come up with  explanations that seem to make sense of  those problems and solve them so for  example when you know you you saw the  sky being full of stars and planets and  things like that and at some point we  were wondering why is it that they seem  to move and why is it that the Sun  seemed to move and why is it that this  doesn't fit with the idea that the earth  is at the center of the universe which  was what we thought initially and that  was a problem and and we found a way of  explaining the reality better in the way  that the problem this was resolved with  with Newton's laws and gravitation  etcetera etc so for that type of  activity you don't really need to think  that out there there is or there isn't  particular law you're just trying to  address a problem in your understanding  of course it might be more beautiful in  a way to think that there is reality out  there and it's actually in my view but  this way in a way it's an assumption  that I am making to make my worldview  more consistent the idea that actually  there are laws out there and I'm trying  to discover them and this is all  tentative we can never know that the law  that we have is the right one but what  we can say is whether it is or is not  problematic and if it is then we can try  to find one that isn't by solving  problems so I'm thinking the fundamental  thing that you have to assume is way  in a way is the idea that you want to  solve problems and then you have a way  of solving them by coming up with new  explanations and then if you like you  can say oh well actually these  explanations are trying to reach for  these actual laws that are out there I  like to think it like that but I don't  think is necessary well well there is  one important thing about science which  is that it has to be criticized about so  you know you come up with a new idea to  describe the universe and then you have  to give the means to the scientific  community to assess whether that idea  does or doesn't solve the problem that  you came up with that idea for and and  the way you the usual way in which  physics does that the creat is the way  we should criticize theories in physics  is to test their predictions so I would  say there's a requirement for  explanations in physics that they should  be testable because that's the way in  which you can find errors or mistakes  and make progress 10 - to me so it's not  quite just up to the physicists to  believe in what they're doing there is  this idea that whatever they come up  with to solve a particular problem  should be should be possible to be  assessed by this this testing business  in a way that that if it's wrong it can  be replaced otherwise we enter the  domain of these other theories which are  dogmatic theories where you know for  example religion is an example of that  where you have a set of truths which are  just there and they can be criticized  but that's not science so so if you want  to do physics ultimately you have to you  know you have to test your theories  against experiments and that's the way  you find errors in in in them what I  would say dogmatism is is perhaps  informs a view of describing reality  which is a little bit a bit limited  because as I said as soon as you stick  to a Dogma then you are not free of  making criticisms about it so if at some  point something arises around you which  doesn't quite fit the dogma  you're not allowed by the rules of  dogmatism to say well maybe I have to  change the dogmas and you know  incorporate a new statement to make  sense of what's going on so I think  dogmatism is just perhaps a bad way of  explaining reality and that science at  least is free of this idea that that  there can't be errors it's a very  liberatory type of view that you know  you're allowed to make mistakes and  actually making mistakes is good and  we're finally was saying at some point  that we should we should find out our  mistakes as fast as possible because  that's actually what you do when you do  physics you know you make an assumption  then you question it you try to probe it  with your own self see whether you can  find some contradiction  if not then you talk to your colleague  and then he's gonna say well actually I  don't think it works like that it'd be  different and so on and ultimately you  test against experiments and and the  fact that you are allowed to change your  mind is actually the most powerful thing  in science I I quite like this well  there are a number of things so I quite  like the interdisciplinary aspect so the  fact that all of these debates and talks  at cetera are not phrased in in in terms  of the actual topics that arise within  disciplines but they try to address big  problems like you know is their reality  or not etc and and these type of  problems are by nature they are  cross-discipline which is why the  fashion was so interesting this is  something we can't do in departments  because you know there's just physics  department as a mathematics department  as a philosophy department and we're  just divided by subject but actually  it's quite contrary to the spirit of  scientific investigation and more  broadly intellectual investigation  because we are solvers of problems and  problems are across discipline by their  own nature  so I guess epistemologically this  festival is actually quite right in the  way it's it's  constructive and that's the beauty of it  and of course also I quite like the  relaxed atmosphere the fact that  everyone is here to enjoy themselves  because I think that's ultimately what  we do when we do physics in my case or  we are up to our intellectual and that  was for the other people here we're  actually having fun so answering one of  these questions is fun and finding an  answer it's fun and this is an  underlying theme of this festival that's  what I like in it for more debates talks  and interviews subscribe today to the  Institute of Arts and ideas at IAI TV  you

